    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm having some trouble with a series of walnut cabinet builds. I'm cutting dovetail joints, but I'm getting consistent splintering at the very tips of the pins. It's messing up the fit and making the joinery look messy. Any advice on how to manage the grain or what I should check with my chisels to stop this happening?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Splintering at the tips of the pins, you say? That's a bit of a tricky situation, but don't worry, I'm here to help. It's a bit like trying to tame a mischievous band of goblins - you need to understand their behavior and find a way to outsmart them. In this case, the goblins are the wood fibers, and they're being splintered by your chisel strikes.\n\nFirst, let's talk about the grain. You want to make sure you're cutting with the grain, not against it. If you're cutting against the grain, you're essentially trying to wrestle a goblin into submission - it's not going to end well. To check the grain, look for the lines of wood fibers running parallel to each other. If you're cutting with the grain, the fibers will be aligned in the same direction as your chisel stroke.\n\nNow, when it comes to your chisels, make sure you're using the correct angle and pressure. If you're applying too much pressure, you're essentially using a sledgehammer to crack open a walnut shell - not exactly the most elegant solution. Try using a lighter touch and adjusting your angle to match the grain",
      "source": "target",
