    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm not really sure what you mean by that comparison, but anyway, I am definitely cutting with the grain. I've checked the orientation multiple times. I've also honed the chisels recently, so I'm pretty confident they're sharp. Even so, the tips just keep blowing out. Could it be related to the moisture content of the walnut, or maybe the way I'm supporting the wood while I par them?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I see what I did there with the goblin comparison - it's a bit of a stretch, I admit. But, moving on to the issue at hand, it's great that you're cutting with the grain and have honed your chisels.\n\nNow, let's talk about the moisture content of the walnut. You see, wood is like a goblin's lair - it's full of hidden dangers. If the wood is too dry, it's like trying to cut through a brittle, dry leaf - it's just not going to work. On the other hand, if the wood is too wet, it's like trying to cut through a soggy, moldy mess - it's a recipe for disaster.\n\nTo check the moisture content, you can use a wood moisture meter. If the reading is too high, it might be causing the wood to split and splinter. As for supporting the wood, make sure you're using a stable, flat surface and keeping the wood taut. You don't want the wood to be sagging or flexing, like a goblin trying to sneak up on you - it's just not going to work.\n\nAlso, try using a paring chisel with a slightly more pronounced",
